== findings ==
1. Scope 0 (1 room, 18 global scripts, 28 units) reads in 0.9 s wall and 5 CPU s: 878 records, 816 actions,
   3,123 operators and 27,855 effects before relevance, a 0.59 MB task file. No record writes owner:901, 902 or
   903, so relevance keeps 0 of 816 actions.

2. Scope 1 (4 rooms, 42 global scripts, 140 units) reads 237,262 records in 1,163 s wall and 7,131 CPU s (6.1 of
   16 CPUs busy on average): 125,909 event, 83,418 sentence, 21,730 skip_cutscene, 4,612 walk_to and 1,593
   dialog_line records. The task has 236,678 actions and 13,513,842 effects. build_task takes 164 s, relevance
   47 s. The reading process peaks at 16.1 GB RSS (in write_sas of the task before relevance), the largest worker
   at 2.9 GB, the machine at 16.1 GB in use above its base. The reading took 2 passes (40 domain values added
   after pass 1) of 1,027 event rounds each; in each pass 518 rounds had 1 or 2 runs, and the 927 rounds past
   round 100 took 30 to 36 s.

3. Scope 1 cannot reach the goal. No record writes owner:901, 902 or 903, so relevance keeps 0 of 236,678 actions
   and the pruned task has 0 operators (424 bytes). The IR agrees: at k=1 the goal is writable only through a
   wildcard owner: write whose object does not fold. The exact writes (pickupObject 901 to 903) are in room 83's
   local script 200, the dock, which enters at k=2.

4. Scope 1's task before relevance cannot be written. task.write_sas stops on record #150,299,
   event(script:47@35), with "pos:local:200 := 10 is not in its domain". Cause (a reader finding; reader.py not
   changed):
   - pos:<unit> takes its values from the stop points of the scope's own units (FactTable.points, from
     Scope.universe). The unit name local:200 is shared by every room's local script 200. At k=1 the domain of
     pos:local:200 is -1 and the stop points of the local 200 of rooms 28, 33, 35 and 38: 41 values, no 10.
   - The room fact's domain is wider than the scope: the scope's rooms, the start room and every constant room
     change in the scope. At k=1 it has 17 values: 0, 28, 29, 33, 34, 35, 38, 41, 63, 70, 72, 79, 81, 82, 83, 85
     and 96.
   - Global script 47 from offset 35 runs startScript(var 193), and var 193's domain is {0, 200, 208}. With room
     41, 79 or 96 and var 193 = 200, the event starts that room's local script 200, which waits at offset 10.
   - Builder.positions turns the write at:local:200:10 = 1 into pos:local:200 := 10 without checking the
     domain, so the reader's own check (keys written outside their domain: 0 at k=1) does not see it and the
     second pass does not widen it. write_sas is the first place that checks.

5. Scope 2 (10 rooms, 82 global scripts, 283 units) stopped at the 60-minute limit of the read step, in pass 1.
   By then it had read 500,605 records from 21,570 runs: inputs 4,702 runs and 163,981 records in 939 s, event
   round 1 5,042 runs and 229,680 records in 1,877 s, event round 2 1,372 runs and 14,603 records in 343 s, and
   41 more event rounds. That is already 2.1 times k=1's whole reading (237,262 records).
   - Memory was not the limit. The machine's memory in use peaked at 52.4 GB above its base (limit 100 GB); the
     reading process at 8.8 GB (sampled); the largest worker at 8.8 GB (sampled).
   - The CPUs were mostly idle: 15,473 CPU s over 3,631 s, 4.3 of 16 CPUs busy on average. In the inputs stage 2
     CPUs were busy for 12 of its 15.6 minutes; in event round 1 all 16 were busy for about 5 minutes, then 1 to 3
     for the last 23 minutes. The stages wait on a few long runs.
   - A stack dump (py-spy) of the last busy worker of event round 1, at 18:39 UTC, showed it in
     event(script:2@721), an event of global script 2, inside a split run (a run past PATH_LIMIT run again per
     value of its first fork).
   - k=2's whole read, if its pass 1 has k=1's shape (the first 44 stages are 85% of pass 1's time) and it needs 2
     passes like k=1: about 4,200 s per pass, 8,400 s (2.3 hours) in all, before build_task. It does not fit in
     one more 2-hour start, so k=2 was not run again.
   - The first attempt of this job (model.py e413983eaf2d) stopped k=2 at the same limit after the same first two
     stages (163,981 and 229,680 records, in 1,165 s and 2,351 s).

6. Growth per added room.
   - k=0 -> k=1 (+3 rooms, +112 units), the whole reading: records x270 (+78,795 per added room), actions x290
     (+78,621 per added room), effects x2,400 (+4.50 million per added room), read CPU s x1,345 (+2,375 per added
     room), peak RSS of the reading process x214 (+5.3 GB per added room).
   - k=1 -> k=2 (+6 rooms, +143 units), at the same depth (pass 1's inputs and event rounds 1 to 43, the stages
     k=2 finished; 94% of k=1's pass 1 records): records 203,434 -> 500,605, x2.46, +49,528 per added room; runs
     12,460 -> 21,570, x1.73; wall 476 s -> 3,582 s, x7.5, +518 s per added room; wall per run 0.038 s -> 0.166 s.
   - The growth is not regular. As a power of the units in the scope, records grow with exponent 3.5 from k=0 to
     k=1 and 1.3 from k=1 to k=2. The read time grows faster than the records: exponent 2.9 on units from k=1 to
     k=2, because each run takes 4.4 times longer. With two points per step there is no fit; the projections
     below are two-point power laws.

7. A full-game scope (83 rooms, 155 global scripts, 1,172 units), if the growth held:
   - records: 3.6 million (k=1 -> k=2 exponent on units; 4.7 million on rooms) to 385 million (k=0 -> k=1
     exponent on units).
   - read wall on 16 workers: about 514,000 s, 143 hours (k=1 -> k=2 exponent on units, from k=1's 1,163 s);
     257 hours on rooms.
   - memory: k=1's reading process peaked at 16,459 MB for 237,262 records, about 69 KB per record. At 3.6 to
     4.7 million records the same rate gives about 245 to 315 GB, two to three times the VM's 125.8 GB.
   - goal: no scope read so far writes the goal; k=2 is the first scope whose code holds the exact goal writes.

8. Not run: k=3 (22 rooms, 91 global scripts, 463 units). Growth stopped at k=2, the first scope that hit a
